## Successor Beneficiaries of an Already-Inherited IRA

If you inherit an IRA from someone who had inherited it themselves, the IRS calls you a successor beneficiary, and you generally do not get a new 10-year window. Your deadline, and whether you must take money out every year, depend on who the first beneficiary was.

When the first beneficiary was on the 10-year rule, you take over whatever time is left on the original owner's clock. The account still has to be empty by December 31 of the 10th year after the original owner's death. If the original owner died on or after their required beginning date, generally age 73, the annual withdrawals generally continue for you through the remaining years.

When the first beneficiary was an eligible designated beneficiary taking withdrawals over life expectancy (for example, a disabled or chronically ill person), your deadline counts from that person's death instead. You generally must empty the account by December 31 of the 10th year after the first beneficiary's death. Until then, you keep taking annual withdrawals on the first beneficiary's remaining life expectancy schedule.

A surviving spouse who rolled the IRA into their own IRA is the third case. After that rollover, the account was the spouse's own IRA, so you are not a successor beneficiary at all. You are a new first-generation beneficiary, with your own 10-year window counted from the spouse's death.

Missing a required withdrawal can trigger the same 25% excise tax covered above, which drops to 10% if you correct it within the IRS correction window. Rules vary by case, and a tax professional can apply them to yours. Before the first withdrawal, take these four steps:

- Get the inherited IRA's account records, including the beneficiary designation form that named you.
- Find the first beneficiary's category, the year the original owner died, and whether that death came before or after the owner's required beginning date.
- Ask the IRA custodian for your withdrawal schedule in writing.
- Name your own successor beneficiary on the inherited IRA so the account does not default to your estate or the custodian's default rules, using our [estate planning](/estate-planning/) guides to fit it into the rest of your plan.